WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:09.989
A Mac has no Cast button. Chrome casts a browser tab, and a phone casts from an app, but neither plays an MP3 from your Mac on the TV with proper controls.

00:10.339 --> 00:11.742
This native player does.

00:12.942 --> 00:16.974
It finds every Chromecast and Google TV on your network, by name.

00:17.324 --> 00:26.424
Open an MP3, pick a TV, and press play. Pause, seek, volume and speed all work, and the controls show what the TV reports.

00:26.774 --> 00:32.025
With ffmpeg installed, the TV shows a moving spectrum of the music instead of a still picture.

00:33.225 --> 00:35.864
A Chromecast never receives your file directly.

00:36.214 --> 00:44.037
The player discovers TVs with macOS's own dns-sd, opens a TLS session, and launches the default media receiver.

00:44.387 --> 00:49.473
Then it serves the file from a small web server on your Mac, and tells the TV where to fetch it.

00:50.673 --> 00:54.282
The whole interface is one data file of state machines and skins.

00:54.632 --> 01:03.326
The only code is this adapter, which answers each request with the Chromecast package. An unknown request becomes an error on screen, never a silent no-op.

01:04.526 --> 01:13.214
Every byte goes through one algebraic effect. In the tests, a handler plays the TV, so four end-to-end journeys run with no window and no network.

01:13.564 --> 01:17.904
The package was also checked by hand on a real Chromecast and a real Google TV.

01:19.104 --> 01:22.160
Build it from source, and run the casting example.

01:22.510 --> 01:26.809
It plays MP3 files only, and it has been tested on macOS.

01:27.159 --> 01:31.123
Osprey is actively evolving. Try it, and tell us what breaks.
